('MPM2D', 'linear-systems', 'number-of-solutions', 20, 'Advanced',
 'How many solutions does the system 4x - 6y = 10 and 6x - 9y = 15 have?', 2,
 '[{"text": "No solution", "feedback": "Check whether the second equation is a consistent multiple of the first — every term here scales by exactly 1.5."}, {"text": "Exactly one solution", "feedback": "The ratio of x-coefficients (4:6) and y-coefficients (-6:-9) both simplify to 2:3 — matching ratios rule out a single intersection point."}, {"text": "Infinitely many solutions", "feedback": "Correct. Every term scales by exactly 1.5 (4×1.5=6, −6×1.5=−9, 10×1.5=15), so the second equation describes the identical line."}, {"text": "Exactly two solutions", "feedback": "A linear system can never have exactly two solutions."}]'::jsonb,
 'solutions-standard-form-ratio-test'),
('MPM2D', 'linear-systems', 'linear-system-applications', 6, 'Easy',
 'Two numbers have a sum of 30. Twice the smaller equals the larger minus 6. Which system represents this, using x for the larger and y for the smaller?', 2,
 '[{"text": "x + y = 30 and x = 2y - 6", "feedback": "This has the larger equalling \"twice the smaller minus 6\" — but the sentence says the *smaller doubled* equals the larger minus 6, not the reverse."}, {"text": "2x + y = 30 and x - y = 6", "feedback": "This misreads \"sum of 30\" as involving a doubled term, and turns \"minus 6\" into a plain difference instead of part of the second equation."}, {"text": "x + y = 30 and 2y = x - 6", "feedback": "Correct. \"Sum of 30\" gives x + y = 30, and \"twice the smaller equals the larger minus 6\" gives 2y = x − 6."}, {"text": "x + y = 30 and y = 2x - 6", "feedback": "This swaps which variable is doubled — it''s the smaller (y) that''s doubled, not the larger (x)."}]'::jsonb,
 'system-setup-translation'),
('MPM2D', 'linear-systems', 'linear-system-applications', 7, 'Easy',
 'A rectangle''s perimeter is 40 cm, and its length is twice its width. Which system represents this, using l for length and w for width?', 1,
 '[{"text": "l + w = 40 and l = 2w", "feedback": "Perimeter is 2l + 2w, not l + w — a rectangle has two lengths and two widths, not just one of each."}, {"text": "2l + 2w = 40 and l = 2w", "feedback": "Correct. The perimeter formula gives 2l + 2w = 40, and \"length is twice its width\" gives l = 2w directly."}, {"text": "2l + 2w = 40 and w = 2l", "feedback": "This swaps which side is doubled — the length is twice the width, not the other way around."}, {"text": "l × w = 40 and l = 2w", "feedback": "Multiplying l and w gives the rectangle''s area, not its perimeter — the first equation should add the sides instead."}]'::jsonb,
 'system-setup-translation'),
('MPM2D', 'linear-systems', 'linear-system-applications', 8, 'Easy',
 'Apples cost $2 each and oranges cost $3 each. Someone bought 12 pieces of fruit in total for $30. Which system represents this, using a for apples and o for oranges?', 0,
 '[{"text": "a + o = 12 and 2a + 3o = 30", "feedback": "Correct. \"12 pieces of fruit in total\" gives a + o = 12, and the total cost gives 2a + 3o = 30."}, {"text": "a + o = 30 and 2a + 3o = 12", "feedback": "The total count (12) and the total cost (30) have been swapped between the two equations."}, {"text": "2a + 3o = 12 and a + o = 30", "feedback": "This also swaps the two totals — the piece count (12) belongs with a + o, and the cost (30) belongs with 2a + 3o."}, {"text": "3a + 2o = 30 and a + o = 12", "feedback": "The prices are swapped — apples cost $2 (not $3) and oranges cost $3 (not $2)."}]'::jsonb,
 'system-setup-translation'),
('MPM2D', 'linear-systems', 'linear-system-applications', 9, 'Easy',
 'One number is 5 more than another. Their sum is 21. Which system represents this, using x for the larger and y for the smaller?', 3,
 '[{"text": "x + y = 5 and x = y + 21", "feedback": "The sum (21) and the difference (5) have been swapped between the two equations."}, {"text": "x - y = 21 and x + y = 5", "feedback": "The sum and the difference are swapped here as well — the sum should equal 21, and the difference should equal 5."}, {"text": "y = x + 5 and x + y = 21", "feedback": "This makes the smaller number (y) the one that''s larger by 5 — it should be x, the larger number, that''s 5 more than y."}, {"text": "x = y + 5 and x + y = 21", "feedback": "Correct. \"One number is 5 more than another\" gives x = y + 5, and \"their sum is 21\" gives x + y = 21."}]'::jsonb,
 'system-setup-translation'),
('MPM2D', 'linear-systems', 'linear-system-applications', 10, 'Medium',
