('MPM2D', 'quadratic-relations', 'quadratic-applications', 14, 'Challenge',
 'The sum of a positive number and its square is 42. What is the number?', 1,
 '[{"text": "7", "feedback": "Check: 7 + 7² = 7 + 49 = 56, not 42 — recheck the factored equation."}, {"text": "6", "feedback": "Correct. Let x be the number: x + x² = 42 gives x²+x−42 = 0, which factors as (x+7)(x−6) = 0; rejecting the negative solution leaves x = 6."}, {"text": "-7", "feedback": "The question asks for the positive number — reject the negative solution."}, {"text": "42", "feedback": "This reuses the given total directly as if it were the number itself — set up and solve x + x² = 42 instead."}]'::jsonb,
 'quadratic-application-reject-negative'),
('MPM2D', 'quadratic-relations', 'quadratic-applications', 15, 'Challenge',
 'A ball''s height is modelled by h = -5t² + 25t. At what time does it hit the ground (other than at the moment it''s thrown)?', 3,
 '[{"text": "t = 0 seconds", "feedback": "This is the moment the ball is thrown (its starting height, 0) — the question asks for when it lands afterward."}, {"text": "t = 25 seconds", "feedback": "This reuses a coefficient directly as if it were the answer — solve -5t²+25t = 0 by factoring instead."}, {"text": "t = -5 seconds", "feedback": "A negative time doesn''t make sense in this context — solve -5t²+25t = 0 and keep only the positive, physically meaningful solution."}, {"text": "t = 5 seconds", "feedback": "Correct. Setting h = 0 gives -5t²+25t = 0, which factors as -5t(t-5) = 0, so t = 0 (the throw) or t = 5 (when it lands)."}]'::jsonb,
 'quadratic-application-zero-height'),
('MPM2D', 'quadratic-relations', 'quadratic-applications', 16, 'Challenge',
 'The product of two consecutive positive integers is 132. What are the two integers?', 0,
 '[{"text": "11 and 12", "feedback": "Correct. Let n be the smaller integer: n(n+1) = 132 gives n²+n−132 = 0, which factors as (n+12)(n−11) = 0; rejecting the negative solution leaves n = 11, so the integers are 11 and 12."}, {"text": "12 and 13", "feedback": "Check: 12 × 13 = 156, not 132 — recheck the factored equation."}, {"text": "-12 and -11", "feedback": "The question asks for positive integers — reject the negative solution."}, {"text": "10 and 11", "feedback": "Check: 10 × 11 = 110, not 132 — recheck the factored equation."}]'::jsonb,
 'quadratic-application-consecutive-integers'),
('MPM2D', 'quadratic-relations', 'quadratic-applications', 17, 'Advanced',
 'A ball''s height is modelled by h = -5t² + 30t + 2. What is its maximum height?', 1,
 '[{"text": "45 m", "feedback": "This is the value of -5t² alone at the vertex — the other two terms (30t and 2) still need to be added in."}, {"text": "47 m", "feedback": "Correct. The vertex occurs at t = -30÷(2×-5) = 3; substituting gives h = -5(3)²+30(3)+2 = -45+90+2 = 47 m."}, {"text": "32 m", "feedback": "This adds only two of the three terms at the vertex — recompute -5(3)²+30(3)+2 in full."}, {"text": "2 m", "feedback": "This is only the ball''s starting height (at t=0) — the maximum height occurs later, at the parabola''s vertex."}]'::jsonb,
 'quadratic-application-max-min'),
('MPM2D', 'quadratic-relations', 'quadratic-applications', 18, 'Advanced',
 'Revenue is modelled by R = -2p² + 80p, where p is the price. What price maximizes revenue, and what is that maximum revenue?', 2,
 '[{"text": "p = 40, R = $1600", "feedback": "Recompute the vertex''s location: p = -80÷(2×-2) = 20, not 40 — recheck the sign and arithmetic."}, {"text": "p = 20, R = $1600", "feedback": "The price is correct, but recompute the revenue at that price: R = -2(20)²+80(20) — combine both terms carefully."}, {"text": "p = 20, R = $800", "feedback": "Correct. The vertex occurs at p = -80÷(2×-2) = 20; substituting gives R = -2(20)²+80(20) = -800+1600 = 800."}, {"text": "p = 80, R = $800", "feedback": "This reuses a coefficient directly as if it were the price — find the vertex using p = -b÷(2a) instead."}]'::jsonb,
 'quadratic-application-max-min'),
('MPM2D', 'quadratic-relations', 'quadratic-applications', 19, 'Advanced',
 'A rectangular field uses 200 m of fencing on three sides (against a barn on the fourth side). Its area is modelled by A = w(200 - 2w), where w is the width. What width maximizes the area, and what is that maximum area?', 3,
 '[{"text": "w = 100, A = 0 m²", "feedback": "At w = 100, the length (200-2w) becomes 0, giving zero area — that''s a minimum, not the maximum."}, {"text": "w = 25, A = 3750 m²", "feedback": "Rewrite A = w(200-2w) as A = -2w²+200w, then find the vertex at w = -200÷(2×-2) — this doesn''t give w = 25."}, {"text": "w = 200, A = 0 m²", "feedback": "A width of 200 would make the length (200-2w) negative, which isn''t physically possible — recheck the vertex calculation."}, {"text": "w = 50, A = 5000 m²", "feedback": "Correct. Rewriting A = -2w²+200w, the vertex occurs at w = -200÷(2×-2) = 50; substituting gives A = 50(200-100) = 50(100) = 5000 m²."}]'::jsonb,
 'quadratic-application-max-min-fencing'),
('MPM2D', 'quadratic-relations', 'quadratic-applications', 20, 'Advanced',
 'A ball thrown from a 2 m platform has height h = -5t² + 20t + 2. At what time does it first reach a height of 17 m?', 0,
 '[{"text": "t = 1 second", "feedback": "Correct. Setting h = 17 gives -5t²+20t+2 = 17, so -5t²+20t-15 = 0; dividing by -5 gives t²-4t+3 = 0, which factors as (t-1)(t-3) = 0 — the ball first reaches 17 m at t = 1 (on the way up), reaching it again at t = 3 (on the way down)."}, {"text": "t = 3 seconds", "feedback": "This is when the ball passes 17 m a second time, on its way back down — the question asks for the first time it reaches that height."}, {"text": "t = 17 seconds", "feedback": "This reuses the target height directly as if it were a time value — solve -5t²+20t+2 = 17 for t instead."}, {"text": "t = 2 seconds", "feedback": "Check this value: substituting t = 2 into -5t²+20t+2 gives 22 m, not 17 m."}]'::jsonb,
